  <!-- HERO -->
  <header class="hero" id="top" data-rail-section="top">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="meta-line">
        <span class="label">Product Designer</span>
        <span class="label">Tel Aviv — 2026</span>
      </div>
      <h1>
        <span class="hero-line"><span>Designing the interface</span></span>
        <span class="hero-line"><span>between people and the</span></span>
        <span class="hero-line"><span>systems that <em>read</em> them.</span></span>
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">Working at the edge of personalization, user agency, and the quiet machinery that decides who you are online. Currently finishing an M.Des at Bezalel.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- WORK -->
  <section id="work">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="meta-line reveal" style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)">
        <span class="label">Selected Work</span>
        <span class="label">Index — 03 Projects</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 01 -->
    <div class="wrap proj" id="user-id-model" data-rail-section="user-id-model">
      <div class="proj-head reveal">
        <span class="label">Work — 01</span>
        <div class="specs-wrap">
          <div class="specs">
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Type</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">B2C · Dashboard · AI</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Discipline</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">UX · UI · Data Viz · Motion</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Year</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">2025 — 2026</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Context</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">M.Des / Bezalel</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Tools</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">Figma · After Effects</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <h2 class="proj-title">User ID Model</h2>
        <p class="proj-lead">A cross-platform interface that surfaces the algorithmic model each platform builds of you — and turns personalization into something you can see and steer.</p>
        <a href="#uim-detail" class="viewlink">View case <span class="arr">↓</span></a>
      </div>

      <div class="proj-visual reveal">
        <div class="panel wide"><!-- EDIT-IMG --><span class="pl">User ID Model — key visual</span><span class="ph">Hero screen / main composition</span></div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="panel"><!-- EDIT-IMG --><span class="pl">Distributed User Model</span></div>
          <div class="panel"><!-- EDIT-IMG --><span class="pl">Your Digital Ecosystem</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="detail" id="uim-detail">
        <div class="pa">
          <div class="reveal"><div class="k">Problem</div><p>Recommendation systems infer an identity for each user and act on it silently. People feel the effects — eerie ads, narrowing feeds — but have no surface to see, question, or correct the model.</p></div>
          <div class="reveal d1"><div class="k">Approach</div><p>Treat the user model as a first-class object: give it a face, a structure, and controls. Flow from a single sign-in into a “distributed” view of the self across services — then make it editable.</p></div>
        </div>

        <div class="block reveal">
          <span class="label">Step 01</span>
          <h3>Entry — one identity, many doors</h3>
          <p>Sign-in uses the providers people already live inside — Google, Apple, Facebook, Microsoft — with a single primary Log In action. From there the interface resolves into a Distributed User Model: the same person, seen through every service at once.</p>
          <div class="panel"><!-- EDIT-IMG --><span class="pl">Login → Distributed User Model</span><span class="ph">Sign-in / transition · 16:10</span></div>
          <div class="cap">Fig. 1 — Sign-in resolving into the distributed user model.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="block reveal">
          <span class="label">Step 02</span>
          <h3>Your Digital Ecosystem</h3>
          <p>A map of the services that hold a piece of you, and what each infers. Every service is labelled and readable without hovering, so the whole picture is graspable before you dive into any node.</p>
          <div class="panel"><!-- EDIT-IMG --><span class="pl">Ecosystem map</span><span class="ph">Labels visible by default, not on hover · 16:10</span></div>
          <div class="cap">Fig. 2 — Each node names its service and the data it reads.</div>
          <div class="note"><b>Reviewer note → fix:</b> persistent service labels (not hover-only), and more than one action per node — <i>view inferences · adjust · disconnect</i> — so “Delete account” isn’t the only move.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="block reveal">
          <span class="label">Step 03</span>
          <h3>Editing the model</h3>
          <p>Each inferred trait becomes a control. Sliders adjust how strongly a signal feeds personalization; a clearly labelled Reset to default restores the platform’s original guess, so nothing is a one-way door.</p>
          <div class="panel"><!-- EDIT-IMG --><span class="pl">Editable traits — sliders</span><span class="ph">Explicit “Reset to default”, not an ambiguous arrow · 16:10</span></div>
          <div class="cap">Fig. 3 — Tuning the signals that shape recommendations.</div>
          <div class="note"><b>Consistency fix:</b> give steps 01–04 one shared container so the flow reads as a single system, not separate frames.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="note reveal"><b>On realism:</b> this is <i>critical / speculative design</i>. The multi-provider sign-in and editable model don’t mirror how auth works today — that gap is the argument. Kept intentionally, with the UX made legible enough to read as a real product.</div>

        <p class="reflection reveal">“The system already has a model of you. This one just lets you look back.”</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap pull reveal">
      <p>Personalization is a portrait drawn behind your back. Design can hand you the mirror.</p>
      <div class="by">— Working note, M.Des research</div>
    </div>

    <!-- 02 -->
    <div class="wrap proj" id="letterboxd" data-rail-section="letterboxd">
      <div class="proj-head reveal">
        <span class="label">Work — 02</span>
        <div class="specs-wrap">
          <div class="specs">
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Type</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">Mobile · Navigation · IA</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Discipline</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">Product · UX · UI</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Year</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">2025</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Platform</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">iOS</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><span class="k">Context</span><span class="fill"></span><span class="v">Self-directed</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <h2 class="proj-title">Letterboxd Redesign</h2>
        <p class="proj-lead">A social network for logging and reviewing films, rebuilt around clearer navigation and a calmer information architecture — keeping the voice, fixing the structure.</p>
        <a href="#lbx-detail" class="viewlink">View case <span class="arr">↓</span></a>
      </div>

      <div class="proj-visual reveal">
        <div class="panel wide"><!-- EDIT-IMG --><span class="pl">Letterboxd — key visual</span><span class="ph">Hero composition</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="detail" id="lbx-detail">
        <div class="note reveal"><b>Context:</b> Letterboxd is a social network for logging, rating, and reviewing films — loved for its taste and tone. This redesign keeps that personality and rebuilds what gets in the way.</div>

        <div class="pa">
          <div class="reveal"><div class="k">Problem</div><p>The app had a clear soul and a confusing body. Key actions were buried, navigation competed with itself, and the most-used flow — logging a film — took more steps than it should.</p></div>
          <div class="reveal d1"><div class="k">Approach</div><p>Audit the information architecture, name the few real problems, and fix them without flattening the voice that makes Letterboxd, Letterboxd. Restraint over reinvention.</p></div>
        </div>

        <div class="block reveal">
          <span class="label">Core flow</span>
          <h3>Log a film — before / after</h3>
          <p>Before and after for each screen in the logging flow, so the reasoning shows step by step. Larger crops than the original deck — legible without zooming.</p>
          <div class="ba" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
            <div><div class="balabel">Before</div><div class="panel tall"><!-- EDIT-IMG --><span class="pl">Log Film — original</span></div></div>
